    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What's the difference between crack filler and crack sealant?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The terms are often used interchangeably for residential products, but technically a \"filler\" is a cold-applied product that fills the void, while a \"sealant\" is a hot-applied, more flexible product engineered to bond to the crack walls and stretch with pavement movement. For most driveways, a quality rubberized filler does both jobs well.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I fill cracks before or after sealing?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Always fill cracks first, then seal. Crack filler is structural and goes into the void; sealer is a thin protective film over the whole surface. Fill and cure your cracks, then apply seal coat over everything for a uniform, fully protected driveway.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does asphalt crack filler last?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A properly applied rubberized or hot-pour filler typically lasts 3 to 5 years. Pourable liquids on the lower end, melt-in ropes and hot-applied sealants on the higher end. Inspect cracks each spring and refresh any that have reopened.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I fill cracks in cold or wet weather?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Crack filler needs a dry crack and surface temperatures above 50°F to bond properly. Moisture trapped inside the crack prevents adhesion and freezes in winter, blowing the repair out. Wait for a dry, mild day and make sure the crack interior is completely dry first.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long should I wait to seal after filling cracks?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Let crack filler cure fully before applying sealcoat over the top—typically 24 to 48 hours for cold-pour products, and longer for deep applications or hot-pour sealants. Sealing over uncured filler traps solvents and leaves a soft spot. When in doubt, give it an extra day; a fully cured filler bonds with the sealcoat for one continuous, durable surface.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need to fill every tiny crack?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not the truly hairline ones under ⅛ inch—those are best handled by a sand-loaded sealcoat that bridges them during application. But any crack you can fit a coin edge into deserves dedicated filler, because that's wide enough for water to reach the base and start the freeze-thaw damage that turns small cracks into expensive potholes.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
