                <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
                <div class="mt-16">
                  <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
                    Common Mistakes When Filling Asphalt Cracks
                  </h2>

                  <p>
                    Crack filling is the highest-value hour of driveway maintenance there is, and it fails almost exclusively for reasons that have nothing to do with which product you bought.
                  </p>

                  <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling a Dirty or Damp Crack</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Filler bonds to the crack walls, and it cannot bond through dirt, moss, or moisture. Blow the crack out with compressed air or a leaf blower, pull out any vegetation by the root, and let it dry completely. This single step separates repairs that last five years from ones that lift in five months.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Pourable Filler on Wide or Deep Cracks</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Poured into a crack more than about half an inch wide, liquid filler sinks, cures hollow, and leaves a depression that collects water. Wide cracks need backer rod or sand packed in first, or a trowel-grade patching compound instead.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overfilling and Leaving a Ridge</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        A mound of filler standing proud of the surface gets caught by snow shovels and plough blades and tears out, taking the repair with it. Fill flush to slightly below the surface and squeegee level — the goal is a sealed crack, not a visible bead.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealcoating Before the Filler Has Cured</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Most cold-pour fillers need 24 to 48 hours before anything goes over them, and hot-applied products need to cool fully. Sealing too soon traps uncured filler, which shows as soft spots and shadow lines through the finished coat.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Closing -->
                <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
                  <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
                  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                    The best <strong class="text-white">asphalt crack filler</strong> is the one matched to the crack in front of you: pourable rubberised filler for hairlines up to about half an inch, trowel-grade patch for anything wider, and a cut-out repair for alligatored areas that no filler will save.
                  </p>
                  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
                    Prep decides the result far more than the product does. Clean the crack out completely, work dry and above 50°F, fill flush rather than proud, and give it a day or two to cure before sealing. Done properly, crack filling is the cheapest way there is to add years to a driveway.
                  </p>
                </div>

